WEBVTT

00:00:03.058 --> 00:00:03.848
Years ago,

00:00:03.929 --> 00:00:05.531
while working at Amazon,

00:00:06.111 --> 00:00:08.155
I had already reached a conclusion.

00:00:09.397 --> 00:00:12.509
A company can afford a lot of desirable jobs

00:00:12.938 --> 00:00:15.585
without needing a team of that size to create

00:00:15.643 --> 00:00:16.862
its business value.

00:00:18.105 --> 00:00:19.974
That judgment stayed with me.

00:00:20.577 --> 00:00:23.213
And as AI takes on more implementation,

00:00:23.782 --> 00:00:27.486
I think engineers should pay closer attention to where

00:00:27.521 --> 00:00:29.216
their work becomes useful.

00:00:30.237 --> 00:00:33.349
Organizations can grow around management layers,

00:00:33.697 --> 00:00:35.114
departmental interests,

00:00:35.415 --> 00:00:37.018
and internal politics.

00:00:37.888 --> 00:00:39.642
Coordination expands.

00:00:40.513 --> 00:00:42.951
The customer's problem may barely change.

00:00:43.763 --> 00:00:47.188
Someone can work very hard while their abilities are

00:00:47.246 --> 00:00:51.205
absorbed by work the organization created for itself.

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:57.312
So Amazon's later restructuring and layoffs didn't surprise me.

00:00:58.495 --> 00:01:00.063
In twenty twenty-four,

00:01:00.632 --> 00:01:05.787
Amazon announced plans to reduce layers and unnecessary processes.

00:01:06.553 --> 00:01:07.621
The following year,

00:01:07.783 --> 00:01:11.789
Andy Jassy said he expected AI efficiency to reduce

00:01:11.835 --> 00:01:14.807
its total corporate workforce over time.

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:20.543
Its January twenty twenty-six layoff announcement again emphasized fewer

00:01:20.659 --> 00:01:23.329
layers and less bureaucracy.

00:01:24.142 --> 00:01:27.334
Those statements describe an organization changing.

00:01:28.031 --> 00:01:31.514
They cannot tell us what any individual contributed.

00:01:32.269 --> 00:01:37.435
My interpretation is that cheaper execution accelerates a reassessment

00:01:37.482 --> 00:01:39.223
that was already needed.

00:01:39.606 --> 00:01:41.824
For the next five to ten years,

00:01:42.149 --> 00:01:45.458
I want to invest more in understanding customers,

00:01:45.841 --> 00:01:47.571
making product decisions,

00:01:47.954 --> 00:01:50.938
and carrying worthwhile work into use.

00:01:51.321 --> 00:01:56.197
Stronger implementation tools give engineers room to take on

00:01:56.313 --> 00:01:58.438
more of that responsibility.

00:01:59.448 --> 00:02:03.140
Boris Cherny has described having Claude Code write his

00:02:03.233 --> 00:02:05.706
code while he continues reviewing it.

00:02:06.658 --> 00:02:10.501
Simon Willison describes quickly trying several prototypes.

00:02:10.942 --> 00:02:14.158
These are particular practitioners' experiences.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.931
What interests me is how they change our allocation

00:02:17.989 --> 00:02:18.674
of time.

00:02:19.847 --> 00:02:22.169
Once you can try an idea more cheaply,

00:02:22.714 --> 00:02:26.104
deciding which idea deserves the effort becomes more valuable.

00:02:27.927 --> 00:02:30.969
The first place I'd invest is a business domain.

00:02:31.514 --> 00:02:33.337
Learn who uses a product,

00:02:33.511 --> 00:02:34.498
who pays,

00:02:34.707 --> 00:02:37.912
and who bears the consequences when it fails.

00:02:38.724 --> 00:02:42.672
Kent Beck used payroll to illustrate how much responsibility

00:02:42.741 --> 00:02:45.957
sits behind apparently simple functionality.

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.963
Calculating an amount is only part of the work.

00:02:50.775 --> 00:02:54.386
Keep learning those obligations through actual projects.

00:02:55.501 --> 00:02:56.383
Over time,

00:02:56.766 --> 00:03:00.737
you'll notice needs that haven't reached a requirements document.

00:03:01.573 --> 00:03:03.581
Then get closer to customers.

00:03:04.614 --> 00:03:07.900
Imagine a tool that processes business documents.

00:03:08.318 --> 00:03:10.292
This is a hypothetical example.

00:03:10.861 --> 00:03:14.053
The demonstration extracts every field beautifully.

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:17.095
But when you watch someone work,

00:03:17.176 --> 00:03:19.127
you discover where they pause:

00:03:20.334 --> 00:03:21.797
an unusual document,

00:03:22.273 --> 00:03:23.736
a missing confirmation,

00:03:24.305 --> 00:03:26.081
a result they don't trust.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:28.937
That pause can tell you more about the next

00:03:29.053 --> 00:03:32.710
feature than another hour polishing the demo.

00:03:33.929 --> 00:03:36.171
Ask the person to show you how they handle

00:03:36.205 --> 00:03:37.320
that case today.

00:03:38.121 --> 00:03:39.143
Who checks it?

00:03:39.642 --> 00:03:41.523
How do they correct a mistake?

00:03:42.614 --> 00:03:45.644
What must continue working in their existing system?

00:03:46.561 --> 00:03:50.683
You may discover that exception handling deserves attention first.

00:03:51.763 --> 00:03:54.468
Use some of the time AI saves to have

00:03:54.491 --> 00:03:55.641
that conversation,

00:03:56.337 --> 00:03:59.530
and come back after delivery to see what changed.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:03.281
The third investment is product judgment.

00:04:04.036 --> 00:04:07.763
Willison says he often tries three design approaches.

00:04:08.657 --> 00:04:12.105
He also advocates watching real people use the software.

00:04:12.859 --> 00:04:15.169
For our imagined document tool,

00:04:15.436 --> 00:04:16.911
compare two approaches:

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:18.792
verify every item,

00:04:19.001 --> 00:04:21.427
or highlight only the exceptions.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.306
Build enough to answer the question.

00:04:25.003 --> 00:04:27.882
Then watch where a user hesitates and whether they

00:04:27.929 --> 00:04:30.007
can finish the original task.

00:04:31.007 --> 00:04:31.971
Before building,

00:04:32.145 --> 00:04:34.514
write down what you need to learn.

00:04:35.327 --> 00:04:36.244
Afterward,

00:04:36.731 --> 00:04:39.019
ask whether the attempt answered it.

00:04:40.087 --> 00:04:42.699
This is how product judgment develops:

00:04:43.268 --> 00:04:44.684
choosing what belongs,

00:04:44.882 --> 00:04:45.868
what can wait,

00:04:46.182 --> 00:04:49.514
and which complexity the business actually requires.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.509
AI can propose alternatives.

00:04:52.997 --> 00:04:55.691
Your contact with the work gives you a basis

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:57.153
for choosing among them.

00:04:57.931 --> 00:05:00.590
The fourth investment is commercial judgment.

00:05:01.484 --> 00:05:04.770
A useful product still asks someone to change.

00:05:05.420 --> 00:05:06.790
Money is one cost.

00:05:07.138 --> 00:05:07.904
Migration,

00:05:08.171 --> 00:05:08.740
learning,

00:05:08.938 --> 00:05:09.785
integration,

00:05:10.192 --> 00:05:11.805
and trust matter too.

00:05:12.688 --> 00:05:16.589
Understand why the customer's existing approach has survived.

00:05:17.541 --> 00:05:21.256
Then test whether your improvement is worth that disruption.

00:05:22.313 --> 00:05:25.761
A working prototype gives you something concrete to discuss

00:05:25.854 --> 00:05:27.618
with a potential customer.

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.648
Would they spend time trying it?

00:05:31.461 --> 00:05:33.400
Would they discuss an integration?

00:05:34.630 --> 00:05:36.035
Who controls the budget,

00:05:36.070 --> 00:05:38.496
and where do they look for alternatives?

00:05:38.914 --> 00:05:40.400
Include model usage,

00:05:40.667 --> 00:05:41.503
maintenance,

00:05:41.666 --> 00:05:43.442
and support in your estimate.

00:05:44.348 --> 00:05:49.479
Cheaper development gives some previously expensive ideas another chance.

00:05:49.897 --> 00:05:53.078
Contact with the market tells you which ones deserve

00:05:53.113 --> 00:05:53.914
to continue.

00:05:55.014 --> 00:05:55.699
Finally,

00:05:56.106 --> 00:05:58.555
take a project through a complete cycle.

00:05:59.577 --> 00:06:04.709
Microsoft Digital reported that faster individual development initially failed

00:06:04.802 --> 00:06:06.857
to improve team productivity.

00:06:07.878 --> 00:06:12.418
It later brought business intent and acceptance criteria into

00:06:12.522 --> 00:06:14.229
a shared specification.

00:06:15.088 --> 00:06:17.201
That's an organizational account,

00:06:17.666 --> 00:06:19.872
but it highlights something practical:

00:06:20.742 --> 00:06:23.598
progress depends on the connections across the work.

00:06:24.307 --> 00:06:25.526
Agree on the goal.

00:06:26.153 --> 00:06:27.545
Connect the systems.

00:06:28.010 --> 00:06:29.705
Check real failure cases.

00:06:30.343 --> 00:06:31.864
Help the user get started,

00:06:31.899 --> 00:06:33.954
and return to learn what happened.

00:06:34.976 --> 00:06:38.575
I recently used AI on a reporting task across

00:06:38.668 --> 00:06:41.698
several systems and verified it in development.

00:06:42.453 --> 00:06:44.844
It made me more willing to take on complete

00:06:44.902 --> 00:06:45.633
tasks.

00:06:46.667 --> 00:06:50.359
Engineering experience helps make the result something others can

00:06:50.417 --> 00:06:50.986
trust.

00:06:51.797 --> 00:06:54.997
The judgment I reached at Amazon still matters to

00:06:55.097 --> 00:06:55.218
me.

00:06:56.678 --> 00:06:59.097
A role can be provided by an organization.

00:07:00.478 --> 00:07:03.857
My abilities need an ongoing connection to real needs.

00:07:05.307 --> 00:07:08.267
Each project is a chance to strengthen that connection:

00:07:09.527 --> 00:07:11.367
understand a customer more deeply,

00:07:12.167 --> 00:07:13.728
make a better product choice,

00:07:14.367 --> 00:07:16.947
and learn what it takes for someone to adopt

00:07:17.007 --> 00:07:17.687
the result.

00:07:18.988 --> 00:07:21.547
If AI can handle much more work in five

00:07:21.607 --> 00:07:22.447
or ten years,

00:07:23.068 --> 00:07:25.867
I want to understand more about what people need

00:07:25.928 --> 00:07:26.447
by then.

00:07:27.327 --> 00:07:30.287
That growth can begin with the next conversation and

00:07:30.347 --> 00:07:31.467
the next project.

00:07:32.807 --> 00:07:36.787
Stronger tools make me optimistic about what engineers can

00:07:36.867 --> 00:07:37.387
attempt.

00:07:38.707 --> 00:07:41.068
I want to use that opportunity to make things

00:07:41.167 --> 00:07:44.427
I care about that someone else actually needs.
